WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:05.296
Imagine a map of the mathematical world.

00:00:05.296 --> 00:00:09.195
It's the&nbsp;
product of thousands of years of human ingenuity,

00:00:09.195 --> 00:00:12.861
from the Babylonians to Riemann to the present&nbsp;
day.

00:00:12.861 --> 00:00:18.901
In this world is everything we know about numbers, shapes and their relationships.

00:00:18.901 --> 00:00:23.868
There are many mathematical continents, each with its own language and culture.

00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:27.236
One&nbsp;
with a distinguished history is number theory.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.520
It's an unpredictable land of&nbsp;
lush forests full of opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.062
with little secrets hidden everywhere.

00:00:34.062 --> 00:00:37.118
Here, they speak the language of arithmetic.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.357
Another continent is called harmonic analysis.

00:00:42.357 --> 00:00:46.949
It's a place of smooth curves, symmetry and repeating patterns.

00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:52.029
Here, mathematical objects&nbsp;
speak the language of signals and waves.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.800
For most of history these two continents&nbsp;
have remained distant strangers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.092
Mathematicians didn't think there would be much&nbsp;
to gain from trying to travel between them.

00:01:01.092 --> 00:01:04.574
But in the last half century something striking has&nbsp;
happened:

00:01:04.574 --> 00:01:09.365
We've discovered glimpses of an enormous bridge that seems to connect the continents.

00:01:09.365 --> 00:01:15.250
As&nbsp;
mathematicians build segments of this bridge, they can pass seemingly intractable problems&nbsp;
across it,

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.438
illuminating them in the process.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.589
The full bridge is called the Langlands&nbsp;
Program.

00:01:22.589 --> 00:01:26.302
It's one of the biggest projects in modern mathematical research.

00:01:26.302 --> 00:01:29.777
Some even like&nbsp;
to call it a grand unified theory of mathematics.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.120
I'm Alex Kontorovich, and I'll be your guide as&nbsp;
we explore some of the patterns and symmetries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.328
suggested by the Langlands Program,

00:01:37.328 --> 00:01:40.624
ideas&nbsp;
that imply deep powerful and beautiful connections

00:01:40.624 --> 00:01:44.687
between the most fundamental&nbsp;
continents of the mathematical world.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.098
in 1967, a 30-year-old-mathematician named&nbsp;
Robert Langlands

00:01:51.098 --> 00:01:54.001
wrote a deceptively modest letter to his colleague,

00:01:54.001 --> 00:01:57.542
the&nbsp;
famous French number theorist Andre Weil.

00:01:57.542 --> 00:02:02.274
"If you are willing&nbsp;
to read this as pure speculation, I would appreciate that," the letter said.

00:02:02.274 --> 00:02:05.174
"If&nbsp;
not, I'm sure you have a wastebasket handy."

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.880
To Weil's surprise, the letter contained a series&nbsp;
of striking conjectures that predicted something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:17.223
very unlikely: A correspondence between two&nbsp;
objects from completely different fields of math.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.369
What Langlands suggested seemed stranger&nbsp;
than telepathy.

00:02:21.369 --> 00:02:27.476
How could two objects indigenous to different mathematical continents&nbsp;
evolved to behave in exactly the same way?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.138
This is the question at the heart of the Langlands&nbsp;
Program.

00:02:32.138 --> 00:02:34.336
To understand this mysterious connection

00:02:34.336 --> 00:02:39.488
we first have to meet two mathematicians who&nbsp;
studied these objects from opposite shores.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.327
This is Srinivasa Ramanujan.

00:02:43.327 --> 00:02:48.009
In 1916, the&nbsp;
self-taught Indian math prodigy got very interested

00:02:48.009 --> 00:02:52.339
in a particular function, now known as&nbsp;
the Ramanujan discriminant function.

00:02:52.339 --> 00:02:57.098
Put simply, this function multiplies infinitely many&nbsp;
terms together.

00:02:57.098 --> 00:03:02.694
In Ramanujan's time, this was already known to be a special&nbsp;
type of function called a modular form.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.440
To see modular forms it's not&nbsp;
enough to look at the real numbers;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.111
we need to treat the input as a complex&nbsp;
number which will produce a complex output.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.478
When we do this, the function starts&nbsp;
to reveal its mesmerizing symmetries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.960
Modular forms are some of the most&nbsp;
bizarre objects in mathematics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.888
They satisfy so many internal symmetries&nbsp;
that it's surprising that they exist at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.913
Ramanujan wasn't the first to study&nbsp;
the symmetries of modular forms,

00:03:32.913 --> 00:03:35.055
but he was the first to imagine

00:03:35.055 --> 00:03:38.756
whether they had any&nbsp;
connection to the distant shores of number theory.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.025
He did this by studying the function from a&nbsp;
different angle.

00:03:43.025 --> 00:03:45.396
What would happen, Ramanujan wanted to know,

00:03:45.396 --> 00:03:49.534
if you multiplied all of its terms&nbsp;
out and collected the coefficients?

00:03:49.534 --> 00:03:54.520
When he did this, Ramanujan noticed that the coefficients&nbsp;
have a special kind of predictive power:

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.728
If you know all of the function's prime coefficients,

00:03:57.728 --> 00:04:00.601
then you can use them to figure out the rest.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.280
For example, if you find this&nbsp;
coefficient, which is negative 24,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.984
and this coefficient, which happens to be&nbsp;
252,

00:04:09.984 --> 00:04:14.188
then I can immediately tell you what the coefficient of x to the sixth will be,

00:04:14.188 --> 00:04:17.860
because&nbsp;
two times three is six.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.121
This pattern always seemed to hold,

00:04:20.121 --> 00:04:23.288
but Ramanujan couldn't prove why.

00:04:23.288 --> 00:04:27.339
He&nbsp;
became obsessed with these numbers, and continued to experiment with them,

00:04:27.339 --> 00:04:30.590
eventually making&nbsp;
more remarkable conjectures on their behavior.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:36.005
But they were so difficult to prove that they&nbsp;
sat collecting dust.

00:04:36.005 --> 00:04:39.780
Nearly six decades later, Belgian mathematician Pierre Delign

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:43.145
came up with&nbsp;
a brilliant proof of Ramanujan's conjecture,

00:04:43.145 --> 00:04:45.105
earning Delign a Fields Medal.

00:04:45.105 --> 00:04:48.325
Delign used&nbsp;
the key insight from Langland's conjectures,

00:04:48.325 --> 00:04:53.082
called functoriality, to bypass the bridge&nbsp;
from harmonic analysis to number theory.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:56.402
But the bridge goes in both directions.

00:04:56.402 --> 00:04:59.472
Now,&nbsp;
it's time to travel over to the shores of number theory,

00:04:59.472 --> 00:05:03.156
where the rest of our story will take&nbsp;
shape.

00:05:03.156 --> 00:05:07.468
In 1637, the French lawyer turned hobbyist mathematician

00:05:07.468 --> 00:05:12.473
Pierre de Fermat scribbled an&nbsp;
equation in his copy of Diophantus's Arithmetica.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.998
He never offered a proof, claiming that it was&nbsp;
too marvelous for the narrow margins to contain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:21.795
The theorem featured a polynomial equation,

00:05:21.795 --> 00:05:25.544
a fundamental object native to the continent of number theory.

00:05:25.544 --> 00:05:28.802
These are some of the&nbsp;
most basic equations you can write down.

00:05:28.802 --> 00:05:32.238
Just variables, with exponents,&nbsp;
that are positive whole numbers.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.178
You may remember the Pythagorean theorem from&nbsp;
school,

00:05:36.178 --> 00:05:40.552
that a right triangle with sides a, b and hypotenuse c

00:05:40.552 --> 00:05:43.392
will satisfy this&nbsp;
equation.

00:05:43.392 --> 00:05:45.482
There are lots of integer solutions.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:50.096
Here's the note that Fermat wrote in the margins&nbsp;
of Arithmetica:

00:05:50.096 --> 00:05:55.882
Unlike the pythagorean theorem, these polynomial equations will never have natural&nbsp;
number solutions,

00:05:55.882 --> 00:05:59.692
except of course if one of the variables is zero.

00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:04.627
This tantalizingly simple&nbsp;
claim came to be known as Fermat's Last Theorem.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:12.306
For 350 years, generations of mathematicians&nbsp;
struggled to prove or disprove this conjecture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:19.285
Then, in the 1990s Princeton mathematician Andrew&nbsp;
Wiles shocked the world with a breakthrough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:26.787
Standing on the shores of number theory, he began&nbsp;
dreaming of a bridge to harmonic analysis.

00:06:26.787 --> 00:06:29.371
To understand how wiles built this bridge,

00:06:29.371 --> 00:06:35.155
we'll&nbsp;
first need to meet a special type of polynomial equation called an elliptic curve.

00:06:35.155 --> 00:06:38.133
Here is an&nbsp;
example of an elliptic curve.

00:06:38.133 --> 00:06:42.126
You'll notice that it only has two variables, x and y.

00:06:42.126 --> 00:06:46.311
Let's&nbsp;
take a look at our elliptic curve graphed on the cartesian plane.

00:06:46.311 --> 00:06:51.020
This shows all the pairs of&nbsp;
real numbers, x and y, that satisfy our equation.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:54.663
But we're not just interested in the real&nbsp;
solutions;

00:06:54.663 --> 00:07:00.902
we also want to make the problem more interesting by restricting the domain to&nbsp;
find solutions in the rational numbers

00:07:00.902 --> 00:07:04.573
(that is, fractions) and in the integers.

00:07:04.573 --> 00:07:09.875
To study these&nbsp;
solutions, it's useful to consider our elliptic curve from another perspective.

00:07:09.875 --> 00:07:13.991
We can use a&nbsp;
fantastic tool from number theory called modular arithmetic.

00:07:13.991 --> 00:07:17.401
It's a way of counting integers using&nbsp;
only the remainders.

00:07:17.401 --> 00:07:21.843
We use this kind of math every day when we tell time on a 12 hour clock.

00:07:21.843 --> 00:07:26.382
If someone says, "Let's meet at 1500," you know they really mean 3 pm,

00:07:26.382 --> 00:07:32.041
because both 15 and 3,&nbsp;
when divided by 12, leave the same remainder.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.539
Now, what if we decided to change our 12 hour&nbsp;
clock to a different number,

00:07:37.539 --> 00:07:39.392
say 31?

00:07:39.392 --> 00:07:42.772
When we do this we change what's called the modulus.

00:07:42.772 --> 00:07:46.269
So when the modulus is 31, what's 6 squared?

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:51.180
Well, it should be 36, but on our clock we see&nbsp;
the number 5

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.876
because we're 5 hours past high noon.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.900
Here's the funny thing about modular&nbsp;
arithmetic:

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:02.240
Equations like this, which don't have rational solutions,&nbsp;
may have modular solutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:06.340
since 6 squared is the same as 5 modular 31

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:09.530
which means that x equals 6 solves the equation.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.564
Now let's go back to our elliptic curve and try to&nbsp;
find modular solutions.

00:08:14.564 --> 00:08:18.696
What we really care about is how many solutions there are.

00:08:18.696 --> 00:08:22.695
Here is the graph&nbsp;
of all of the modular solutions of our equation.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:29.159
The dots are the pairs x and y for which x cubed&nbsp;
plus x

00:08:29.159 --> 00:08:35.972
plus 17 when divided by 31 leaves the same remainder as y squared.

00:08:35.972 --> 00:08:40.510
For a modulus n, let's&nbsp;
call the number of solutions b sub n.

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:45.030
For example, when n equals 31, we have 24 solutions.

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:49.356
We can&nbsp;
compute these values b sub n for lots of different moduli n,

00:08:49.356 --> 00:08:51.417
that is, clocks with different&nbsp;
hours.

00:08:51.417 --> 00:08:55.117
This gives us an infinite sequence.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:03.697
Now we can make a function by taking this infinite&nbsp;
sequence, multiplying each term by a power of x,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.279
and summing them all up.

00:09:06.279 --> 00:09:09.066
The result is called an&nbsp;
infinite power series,

00:09:09.066 --> 00:09:13.007
which is like a polynomial but with an infinite number of terms.

00:09:13.007 --> 00:09:17.304
Right&nbsp;
about now you might be wondering, "What's the point of making this function?"

00:09:17.304 --> 00:09:21.106
When Wiles&nbsp;
began building the bridge to harmonic analysis,

00:09:21.106 --> 00:09:29.027
he used some important scaffolding built decades&nbsp;
earlier by three mathematicians named Taniyama, Shimura and Weil.

00:09:29.027 --> 00:09:34.442
They had predicted that for any&nbsp;
elliptic curve, the resulting function would be a modular form,

00:09:34.442 --> 00:09:40.452
an object with the same kinds&nbsp;
of symmetries that Ramanujan studied In the land of harmonic analysis.

00:09:40.452 --> 00:09:44.202
So since we just&nbsp;
created a function from an elliptic curve,

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:47.733
shouldn't it be a modular form in disguise?

00:09:47.733 --> 00:09:52.245
Remember that Ramanujan started with a function that he knew was a modular form

00:09:52.245 --> 00:09:54.987
and tried to&nbsp;
learn something about its coefficients.

00:09:54.987 --> 00:10:02.380
Here, we have a function and we know its coefficients,&nbsp;
since they come from counting solutions on the elliptic curve with different clocks.

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:08.080
But what we&nbsp;
don't know is whether our function is, in reality, a modular form.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:11.844
So let's graph it on&nbsp;
the unit disk and see what happens.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.429
Incredibly, it does seem to be a modular form.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:19.758
But is this just a fluke?

00:10:19.758 --> 00:10:24.160
This is the question&nbsp;
Wiles had to answer to finish building the bridge.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:29.881
He had to prove that every elliptic curve&nbsp;
is intimately related to a modular form.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:31.863
But wait a second,

00:10:31.863 --> 00:10:33.874
why are we talking about&nbsp;
elliptic curves

00:10:33.874 --> 00:10:37.164
when we started out trying to understand Fermat's last theorem,

00:10:37.164 --> 00:10:40.134
which is a&nbsp;
completely different type of polynomial equation?

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:44.503
German mathematician Gerhard Frey made&nbsp;
a key connection here.

00:10:44.503 --> 00:10:49.078
He observed that if Fermat's equation did have a hypothetical&nbsp;
solution,

00:10:49.078 --> 00:10:54.880
in other words, a counter example, that is a to the p plus b to the p was indeed&nbsp;
equal to c to the p

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:56.356
for p greater than two,

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:00.005
then you could make this elliptic curve,

00:11:00.005 --> 00:11:03.113
which&nbsp;
seems to have extremely bizarre properties.

00:11:03.113 --> 00:11:07.066
In particular, when you create the&nbsp;
infinite power series out of it,

00:11:07.066 --> 00:11:12.714
it would not have the symmetries required to be&nbsp;
a modular form.

00:11:12.714 --> 00:11:15.804
This means that if Fermat's last theorem were false,

00:11:15.804 --> 00:11:19.182
then the Taniyama-Shimura-Weil&nbsp;
conjecture would also be false,

00:11:19.182 --> 00:11:26.353
because the counter-example to Fermat's last theorem would&nbsp;
make an elliptic curve which is not modular.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:33.047
So when Wiles and his student Richard Taylor&nbsp;
proved that every elliptic curve

00:11:33.047 --> 00:11:36.741
does indeed produce an infinite power series which is modular,

00:11:36.741 --> 00:11:40.527
they also showed that Frey's elliptic curve can't exist

00:11:40.527 --> 00:11:44.378
-- which means that a solution to fermat's&nbsp;
equation can't exist either!

00:11:44.378 --> 00:11:47.803
And that's how Wiles proved Fermat's last theorem.

00:11:47.803 --> 00:11:52.459
It's one of&nbsp;
the craziest proofs by contradiction ever.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:57.280
By showing that elliptic curves&nbsp;
give rise to modular forms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:00.960
Wiles built the bridge from number&nbsp;
theory to harmonic analysis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:05.440
And by studying the special predictive&nbsp;
power of the coefficients in modular forms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:10.848
Ramanujan (and later Delign) used the bridge&nbsp;
from harmonic analysis to number theory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:16.059
These proofs are some of the most monumental&nbsp;
achievements in modern mathematics,

00:12:16.059 --> 00:12:22.189
but they're also just a tiny sliver of the vast construction&nbsp;
project that is the Langlands Program.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:27.541
Langland's ideas have traveled to the shores&nbsp;
of algebraic geometry,

00:12:27.541 --> 00:12:29.215
representation theory

00:12:29.215 --> 00:12:34.448
and quantum physics, where mathematicians&nbsp;
will be building bridges for years to come.

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:37.754
Nobody knows how far Langland's vision extends,

00:12:37.754 --> 00:12:44.517
but many mathematicians agree that it has the potential to solve some of the most intractable&nbsp;
problems of our time.

00:12:44.517 --> 00:12:50.650
Someday the Langland's Program may reveal the deepest symmetries between&nbsp;
many different continents,

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:54.367
a kind of grand unified theory of the mathematical world

00:12:54.367 --> 00:13:03.360
that answers the&nbsp;
most fundamental questions we have about numbers.

